diff --git a/tools/hy-batch/README.md b/tools/hy-batch/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..95cf21d781052115a7bb34c4702c0bd24159a5dd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/hy-batch/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# Hy-MT native batch translator
+
+This private tool translates UTF-8 JSONL with the embedded Hy-MT2-1.8B chat template. Each record has `id`, `text`, and `target_lang`. It uses the same English translation prompt, full prompt repetition history, and sampling settings as this workspace's HTTP client.
+
+```powershell
+.\build\optimized\bin\hy-batch.exe `
+  -m models/Hy-MT2-1.8B-NVFP4-fused.gguf `
+  --input requests.jsonl --output translated.jsonl `
+  --parallel 64 --summary results/native-summary.json
+```
+
+CPU sampling is the default. `--backend-sampling` enables sampler offload. `--greedy` provides a deterministic sampling rule for regression checks, although different GPU batch shapes can still affect floating-point ties. Request number `i`, starting at zero, uses `--seed + i`; changing wave width does not change request seeds.
+
+`--gpu-prefix` runs penalties and Top-K+1 as a matrix on CUDA, then runs the existing CPU Top-P, temperature and random sampler. Ties and nonfinite candidates fall back to the full CPU chain with raw logits. Unsupported chains or layouts use the original path. This is distinct from full `--backend-sampling`. The workspace `translate-batch.cmd` wrapper selects the GPU prefix by default; its `--cpu-sampling` option restores the CPU path.
+
+`--parallel` supports 1 through 256 sequences, up to the physical batch size. For 256 sequences, `--context 1024` reduces KV memory; each prompt plus `--max-tokens` must fit that per-sequence limit. `--sampling-threads 1` is the serial baseline and default. Values 2, 4, or 8 use persistent CPU workers on independent sampler chains. The main thread prepares every borrowed output snapshot, waits for all workers, then accepts tokens and resumes decoding. No worker accesses the model context. Parallel sampling requires snapshots, so it cannot be combined with `LLAMA_HYMT_SAMPLING_SNAPSHOT=0` or the wrapper's `--disable-snapshot`. Measure thread counts before choosing a deployment default.
+
+By default, the main thread obtains all active snapshots with one synchronization/reorder call, including when `--sampling-threads 1` samples them sequentially. `LLAMA_HYMT_BATCH_SNAPSHOT=0` or the wrapper's `--disable-batch-snapshot` restores individual access for comparison. Disabling snapshots entirely with one sampling thread also preserves the legacy path.
+
+The tool pre-fills independent prompts and then decodes one token for every slot at each step. Completed slots continue with private dummy EOS tokens until the entire wave finishes. A short final CPU wave uses only its actual request count; for example, 129 requests with `--parallel 128` run as widths 128 and 1. `--pad-final-wave` restores full-width padding for comparison. Backend sampling currently always pads the final wave to retain all sampler objects referenced by cached graphs. The summary records `wave_widths`, `final_wave_width`, and `pad_final_wave_effective`. Dummy tokens never enter an active request's attention or repetition history. Different prompt lengths and growing KV-cache dimensions can still require graph updates; a fixed wave does not guarantee that every CUDA graph is reused.
+
+Each output record includes translated `text` and `translation`, source text, ID, prompt count, sampled `completion_tokens`, text `output_tokens`, and `finish_reason` (`stop` or `length`). Completion counts include terminal EOG; text counts exclude EOG. Neither includes padding. Exit code 2 means a translation was truncated or empty; exit code 1 means a processing error.
+
+GPU prefix sampling also retains a full-width final wave for sampler lifetime safety. The optional CPU-only `--continuous --refill-min N` refills free slots after all old outputs have been sampled, clears each reused KV sequence, and creates a new independent sampler. It preserves global request order and seeds, and shrinks trailing idle slots after EOF. This experiment cannot be combined with either GPU sampling option. In measured translation workloads it did not beat the default fixed-wave GPU prefix.
+
+`gpu_prefix_stats` counts processed rows, CPU fallback rows, candidate D2H bytes and raw-logit fallback D2H bytes. These are not output token counts and exclude history H2D. `decode_width_counts` records actual widths in continuous mode; `wave_widths` records initial waves. Snapshot timing includes waiting for GPU outputs, not just CPU work.
+
+`wall_s` excludes model/context initialization and includes JSONL input/output, template rendering, tokenization, prompt evaluation, sampling, and decoding. First-use kernel and graph warm-up remain inside this interval. No warm-up run is silently excluded. The summary separately records dummy decode work and total initialization time.
+
+The `llama_perf` object copies existing runtime counters without extra timing calls. Times are milliseconds. The runtime counts any synchronized batch with more than one token as `p_eval`, including batched generation and padding, and clamps reported token counts to at least one. These counters cannot separate prompt time from generation time in this tool and are not pure GPU timings.
+
+For the exact HTTP benchmark cases, round ordering, and seed progression:
+
+```powershell
+.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts/run_native_experiment.py `
+  --parallel 64 --rounds 4 --cases 64 --label native-nvfp4-c64
+```
+
+Use `--dry-run` to inspect that wrapper's command without loading a model. A new label is required for each run to prevent mixing stale results with a failed run. The HTTP harness uses separate warm-up requests, while native runs include first-use warm-up; retain that distinction when reporting performance.
